+\section*{Supplementary Note 21: conditional whole-image density}
+The matched experiment tests a latent expert shared across a complete native
+image block. Let $h_t$ be the visual transformer's causal state after the
+visible prefix and let $y\in\{0,1\}^{2048}$ denote the next $32\times64$
+bitmap, with one denoting white in this native interface. The normalized law is
+\begin{equation}
+ p_\theta(y\mid h_t)=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{\theta k}(h_t)
+ \prod_{i=1}^{2048}q_{\theta ki}(h_t)^{y_i}
+ [1-q_{\theta ki}(h_t)]^{1-y_i},
+ \qquad q_{\theta ki}=\sigma(a_{\theta ki}),
+\end{equation}
+where the gate is a softmax. Each expert is a neural function of the current
+visual state, without a fixed character identity or stored target bitmap.
+Marginalizing the expert retains a proper probability law. For distinct
+pixels, conditional covariance is
+\begin{equation}
+ \operatorname{Cov}(Y_i,Y_j\mid h_t)
+ =\sum_k\pi_k q_{ki}q_{kj}
+ -\Bigl(\sum_k\pi_k q_{ki}\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_k\pi_k q_{kj}\Bigr).
+\end{equation}
+It can be nonzero even though pixels are independent within one expert.
+This elementary property permits coherent alternatives; it does not prove
+that training discovers valid writing or useful answers.
+
+The objective is exact negative log joint likelihood, computed with
+\texttt{logsumexp}, divided by 2,048 physical pixels. It averages only
+answer and two blank termination blocks within each example, then averages
+over accumulated examples. Teacher forcing uses the same one-block causal
+shift as Note 20. Prompt pixels enter the context without a prompt loss.
+There is no transcript, answer-span offset or Unicode target inside the actor.
+
+The single-head control has 477,145,984 parameters; eight experts have
+489,998,208. Both initialize from the same externally pretrained MIXAR
+revision and use the unchanged Noto-26 cell interface, 3,211 training
+questions and 1,321 article-disjoint development questions. The eight-head
+projection repeats the released head with zero-mean perturbations of scale
+0.01, seed 20266210, and a zero-initialized gate. Every backbone and head
+parameter receives gradients. The comparison matches examples, optimization
+and update budget, rather than parameter count or output computation.
+
+Each arm uses 1,000 updates, microbatch one, accumulation eight, AdamW at
+$2\times10^{-5}$ with betas $(0.9,0.95)$, decay 0.01, 50-update warmup,
+cosine decay to 10\%, norm clip one and EMA 0.99. Weights, optimizer and EMA
+are FP32; forward/backward use BF16 with deterministic algorithms, eager/math
+attention and nonreentrant activation checkpointing. Training and data seeds
+are 20266220 and 20266221. Complete sampled-example orders must match exactly.
+Only the final EMA is audited. Both arms run sequentially on physical GPU 0,
+under a 20 GiB allocated-memory and four-hour ceiling per phase.
+
+Generation thresholds each expert into one complete candidate bitmap, scores
+all candidates under the full mixture and emits the highest-scoring candidate.
+Searching component modes is approximate MAP; it is not a guarantee of the
+global mixture mode. Actual emitted pixels become subsequent context. The
+first exactly white native block ends generation, otherwise ten blocks cap
+the continuation. Only the detected terminal block and wholly blank trailing
+cells are removed. Malformed nonblank strokes are preserved. The deployed
+bundle has no tokenizer, OCR, glyph inventory or external language-model call.
+
+The primary audit retains the fixed 256 questions, blank-question controls,
+all 393 shared-paragraph pairs and all-development likelihood. Paired
+intervals resample articles 2,000 times, seed 20266229. An intervention pass
+requires positive lower intervals for both K8-minus-K1 strict-raster F1 and
+K8 normal-minus-blank F1, fewer than 20\% capped outputs and all implementation
+checks. Each arm's separate bounded reading gate requires at least 10\%
+exact answers, 25\% raster F1, 2\% both-correct pairs, a positive question
+effect, fewer than 20\% caps and implementation checks. Expert-use counts
+and gate entropy are descriptive; they cannot select a new checkpoint or
+change these gates. Reused development, one seed, the shifted font interface
+and external pretraining limit the conclusions.
+
+Both arms complete their registered budget and all replay checks. K=1 takes
+1,887.5 s at 9.904 GiB peak allocated memory; K=8 takes 1,972.8 s at 10.174 GiB.
+Their complete training-example orders match exactly. The locked audit takes
+527.4 s excluding model loading, at 1.904 GiB allocated memory. Four-update
+uninterrupted versus two-plus-resumed-two preflights match model, EMA,
+optimizer and every RNG state exactly for each arm. Future-pixel changes
+have zero effect on earlier predictions. Strict-FP32 cache errors remain
+below $2.1\times10^{-5}$; larger TF32 discrepancies are retained separately
+rather than hidden by changing the production recipe.
+
+All-development NLL is 0.1542 for K=1 and 0.1248 for K=8. Primary strict-raster
+F1 is 0.234\% versus 0.815\%, a 0.580-point gain [0.089, 1.094]. Foreground
+F1 is 12.205\% versus 15.827\%, a 3.623-point gain [1.815, 5.554]. Both
+arms have zero exact primary answers and zero both-correct pairs. K=8's
+normal-minus-blank raster difference is 0.295 points [$-0.006$, 0.623]; its
+foreground difference is $-0.044$ points [$-0.629$, 0.509]. Neither resolves
+question dependence. Seven of eight intervention gates pass; both arms pass
+six of ten bounded reading gates. The registered decision is
+\texttt{reject\_density\_intervention}; neither arm qualifies as free reading.
+
+K=8 selects experts 0, 1, 2, 4 and 7 on respectively 223, 4, 106, 605 and 3
+primary generation steps, including termination. Mean gate entropy is 1.812
+nats, compared with $\log 8=2.079$ for a uniform gate. The primary outputs all
+terminate before the cap, while one blank-question output reaches the cap.
+These descriptive observations do not identify semantic expert roles or
+justify selecting different parameters. The result supports improved bitmap
+fit at this budget, while the required question-dependent language remains
+unestablished.
+
